// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["viii"] = {"qs": [["Sales Practices and Investor Protection", "A client asks the broker's dealer to trade on his behalf without specific instructions each time. The dealer should:", ["Trade only with valid written authorisation", "Trade freely, because the client trusts him", "Use the client's account to trade for other clients too", "Refuse to send any trade confirmation to the client"], 0, "Discretionary trading without proper written authorisation is unauthorised trading. Even with authorisation, every trade must be reported to the client and limited to that client's account."], ["Introduction to Forwards and Futures", "At expiry of a futures contract, the futures price and spot price of the underlying:", ["Diverge by the full cost of carry", "Converge, so the basis becomes approximately zero", "Remain apart by the amount of the initial margin", "Differ by the dividend expected next year"], 1, "As time to expiry shrinks, the cost of carry falls to zero, so the futures price converges to the spot price and the basis disappears. Arbitrage ensures this convergence on the final settlement day."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "Under the Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956, derivatives traded on recognised stock exchanges are:", ["Included within the definition of 'securities'", "Excluded from the definition of 'securities'", "Treated as bank deposits", "Classified as insurance contracts"], 0, "The SCRA was amended to include derivatives in the definition of securities, making exchange trading in derivatives legal and bringing them under SEBI's regulatory oversight."], ["Introduction to Options", "A call buyer pays a premium of ₹45 for a strike of ₹1,500. Ignoring costs, at what expiry price does he break even?", ["₹1,455", "₹1,500", "₹1,590", "₹1,545"], 3, "For a long call, break-even = strike + premium = 1,500 + 45 = ₹1,545. Above this level he makes a net profit. ₹1,455 would be the break-even for a long put with the same strike and premium."], ["Introduction to Forwards and Futures", "Other things equal, if a stock is expected to pay a large dividend before the futures expiry, the fair futures price will be:", ["Higher than it would be without the dividend", "Lower than it would be without the dividend", "Exactly equal to the spot price", "Unaffected, because dividends go to the futures buyer"], 1, "In the cost-of-carry model, income from holding the underlying reduces the carry, so fair value = spot + interest − dividends. A futures buyer does not receive the dividend, so the futures price adjusts down."], ["Understanding Index", "Which corporate action normally requires an index divisor adjustment in a free-float market-cap index?", ["A normal daily rise in the price of a constituent", "A change in the company's statutory auditor", "A rights issue by a constituent company", "A constituent's announcement of quarterly results"], 2, "A rights issue changes the share count and hence market cap without a pure price move, so the divisor is adjusted to keep the index continuous. Daily price changes are what the index is meant to capture."], ["Sales Practices and Investor Protection", "SEBI's investor charter for stock brokers primarily aims to:", ["Fix the brokerage that every broker must charge", "Guarantee profits on all trades placed through a broker", "Inform investors of their rights and redressal options", "List the stocks that investors are forbidden to buy"], 2, "The investor charter sets out the vision, services, rights, responsibilities and grievance redressal steps for investors dealing with brokers. It does not fix brokerage or guarantee returns."], ["Sales Practices and Investor Protection", "Why must brokers send contract notes to clients after derivatives trades?", ["To advertise new products to the client", "To give a legal record of executed trades and charges", "To request additional brokerage from the client", "To inform the client of the next day's market direction"], 1, "A contract note is the legal confirmation of executed trades, showing price, quantity, time and charges, and helps clients verify transactions and detect unauthorised trades."], ["Strategies using Equity Futures and Options", "A calendar spread using calls typically involves:", ["Selling a near-month and buying a far-month call, same strike", "Buying and selling calls of different strikes in the same month", "Buying a call and a put of the same strike and month", "Selling calls on two different underlying stocks"], 0, "A calendar (time) spread uses the same strike but different expiries, aiming to profit from faster time decay in the near-month option. Different strikes in the same month form a vertical spread, not a calendar spread."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "An arbitrageur notices that a stock's futures price is well below its theoretical fair value. Which trade locks in a riskless profit, ignoring costs?", ["Buy the futures and sell the stock in the cash market", "Buy the stock in the cash market and sell the futures", "Buy both the stock and the futures together", "Sell the futures and wait for the price to fall"], 0, "When futures are cheap relative to fair value, the arbitrageur buys the underpriced futures and sells the relatively expensive spot, unwinding at expiry when prices converge. Buying spot and selling futures is the trade for overpriced futures."], ["Trading Mechanism", "A trader places a sell order with a trigger price of ₹490 and a limit price of ₹488 on a stock futures contract trading at ₹500. This order will:", ["Be executed immediately at ₹500", "Be executed only if the futures price first rises back up to ₹490", "Activate at ₹490, then sell at ₹488 or better", "Sell at any price once the market opens"], 2, "A stop-loss sell order lies dormant until the trigger is hit. Once the price falls to ₹490, it becomes a limit order to sell at ₹488 or higher, protecting the trader from further losses."], ["Clearing and Settlement System", "Why do brokers collect margins from clients upfront rather than after the trade?", ["Exchange rules require it and penalise short collection", "Clients can only pay margins after their trades make a profit", "Margins are a fee paid to the broker for executing trades", "Upfront margins are collected only from institutional clients"], 0, "SEBI and exchange norms require brokers to collect initial margins from clients before trading, and short or non-collection attracts penalties on the member. Margins are collateral, not fees."], ["Understanding Index", "Lower impact cost for the stocks in an index is desirable for derivatives trading mainly because it:", ["Guarantees that the index will rise over time", "Reduces the number of stocks needed in the index", "Makes index arbitrage and hedging cheaper to execute", "Removes the need for margins on index futures"], 2, "Low impact cost means large trades in the constituents can be executed close to quoted prices, so arbitrageurs and hedgers can replicate the index cheaply. This keeps futures prices close to fair value."], ["Clearing and Settlement System", "An option buyer pays the premium on the trade. When is the premium normally settled under current Indian practice?", ["Upfront, at the time of the trade", "Only at expiry, if the option is exercised", "Daily, as mark-to-market on the option's value", "It is never paid if the option expires worthless"], 0, "Option buyers must pay the premium upfront; brokers collect it before or at the time of the trade. Options are not marked to market daily like futures; the buyer's maximum loss is this premium."], ["Introduction to Forwards and Futures", "A trader buys Nifty futures and holds them overnight. The next day, the settlement price falls. What happens to his account?", ["Nothing happens until he closes the position", "His initial margin is refunded and the position is closed", "His account is debited with the mark-to-market loss for the day", "He receives the dividend on index stocks to offset the loss"], 2, "Futures positions are marked to market daily: losses are debited and gains credited based on the daily settlement price. The position remains open unless he squares it off or fails to meet margin requirements."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "Why is F&O trading on a recognised stock exchange treated differently from speculative business for tax purposes?", ["Because derivatives never involve any delivery of shares", "Because SEBI collects income tax on F&O trades on behalf of the government", "Because the law expressly excludes them from speculative transactions", "Because all F&O profits are fully exempt from tax"], 2, "Income-tax law expressly carves out eligible derivatives transactions on recognised exchanges from the speculative category, so they are non-speculative business. Profits are taxable, not exempt."], ["Introduction to Forwards and Futures", "A trader is long futures on 250 shares bought at ₹820. Settlement prices are ₹832 on day 1 and ₹815 on day 2. What is the mark-to-market amount on day 2?", ["Loss of ₹1,250", "Gain of ₹3,000", "Loss of ₹4,250", "Loss of ₹1,750"], 2, "Day 2 MTM compares the day 2 settlement price with the day 1 settlement price: (815 − 832) × 250 = −₹4,250. The cumulative loss from the purchase price is ₹1,250, but that is not the day 2 MTM figure."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "Which risk is substantially lower in exchange-traded derivatives than in OTC derivatives, because of the clearing corporation?", ["Market price risk", "Basis risk", "Volatility risk", "Counterparty default risk"], 3, "The clearing corporation becomes the counterparty to every trade through novation and guarantees settlement, so default risk falls sharply. Market, basis and volatility risks come from price behaviour and remain regardless of where the contract trades."], ["Trading Mechanism", "During the ban period for a stock in the F&O segment, a trader with an existing long futures position can:", ["Freely increase the position", "Only reduce the position, not increase it", "Neither reduce nor increase the position", "Convert it into an options position at no cost"], 1, "When a stock's open interest crosses the market-wide position limit, it enters the ban period: fresh positions that increase exposure are not allowed, but existing positions can be reduced or closed. Breaching this attracts penalties."], ["Trading Mechanism", "A trader places an order that must be executed immediately in full or partially, with any unexecuted part cancelled at once. This is an:", ["Good Till Cancelled order", "Stop-loss order", "Immediate or Cancel (IOC) order", "Day order"], 2, "An IOC order executes whatever quantity is available immediately and cancels the rest. A day order stays in the book till the end of the day, and a stop-loss order activates only when a trigger price is reached."], ["Introduction to Options", "In the Black-Scholes model, which input is NOT directly observable in the market and must be estimated?", ["Current price of the underlying", "Volatility of the underlying's returns", "Strike price of the option", "Time remaining to expiry"], 1, "Spot price, strike and time to expiry are known. Volatility must be estimated, either historically or by backing it out of market prices as implied volatility, which is why it is the key uncertain input."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "Which accounting treatment applies to the initial margin deposited by a client with the broker?", ["It is booked as an expense in the year it is paid", "It is shown as income of the client", "It is treated as part of share capital", "It is shown as a current asset, being recoverable"], 3, "Initial margin is a deposit, not a cost, so it remains a current asset until returned. Only actual MTM losses, premiums lost and charges are expenses."], ["Strategies using Equity Futures and Options", "An investor owns shares at ₹400, buys a ₹380 put for ₹8 and sells a ₹440 call for ₹8. Ignoring costs, what is his maximum loss per share?", ["₹20", "₹28", "₹12", "₹8"], 0, "This zero-cost collar has a net premium of nil. If the stock falls below ₹380, the put lets him sell at ₹380, so maximum loss = 400 − 380 = ₹20. Gains are capped at ₹40 by the short call."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "An option buyer pays a premium of ₹20,000 and the option expires worthless. In the books, this premium is:", ["Carried forward as an asset indefinitely", "Refunded by the clearing corporation", "Written off as a loss in the profit and loss account", "Added to the cost of shares in the portfolio"], 2, "Option premium paid is shown as an asset while the position is open. If the option lapses unexercised, the premium has no remaining value and is charged to profit and loss as a loss."], ["Strategies using Equity Futures and Options", "Short selling a stock's futures while buying the stock in the cash market, when futures are overpriced, is known as:", ["Reverse cash-and-carry arbitrage", "Cash-and-carry arbitrage", "Calendar spread trading", "Pair trading"], 1, "In cash-and-carry arbitrage, the trader buys the spot, sells the expensive futures and holds until expiry, earning more than the financing cost. Reverse cash-and-carry is used when futures are cheap."], ["Strategies using Equity Futures and Options", "A trader sells a ₹2,000 put and receives ₹35. He is effectively:", ["Protecting a stock he already owns from a fall", "Betting on a large rise in the stock's implied volatility", "Locking in a sale price of ₹2,035 for his shares", "Ready to buy the stock at a net ₹1,965 if assigned"], 3, "If the stock falls below ₹2,000, the put writer must buy at ₹2,000, but the ₹35 premium lowers the effective cost to ₹1,965. Selling puts is a bullish-to-neutral, short-volatility position."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "A jeweller expects to buy gold in three months and fears a price rise. Using derivatives, she is acting as a hedger if she:", ["Sells gold futures to profit from an expected fall", "Buys gold futures to lock in her purchase cost", "Buys gold futures purely because she expects prices to rise", "Buys gold in one market and sells it in another at the same time"], 1, "A hedger uses derivatives to offset an existing or expected exposure. Buying futures fixes the cost of a future purchase. Taking a view without exposure is speculation, and simultaneous buy-sell for a price gap is arbitrage."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "A trader who trades F&O as a business must maintain books and may require a tax audit when:", ["Statutory turnover or profit thresholds are met", "He trades even a single option contract", "His broker is located outside his city", "He has made a profit in every month of the year"], 0, "Tax audit applicability depends on statutory turnover and profit thresholds; F&O turnover is computed in a specific way. One contract alone does not trigger an audit, and location of the broker is irrelevant."], ["Clearing and Settlement System", "Final settlement of stock futures that remain open at expiry in India is done by:", ["Cash settlement at the closing price of the index", "Physical delivery of the underlying shares", "Rolling over automatically to the next month", "Cancellation without any settlement"], 1, "Stock futures and stock options open at expiry are physically settled: the long receives shares and pays the settlement value. Index derivatives are cash settled because the index cannot be delivered."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "What does 'leverage' in a futures position mean for a trader?", ["A small margin controls a large contract value", "The trader pays the full contract value upfront and earns interest on it", "Losses are capped at the margin deposited with the broker", "The exchange guarantees a minimum return on every position"], 0, "Futures require only a margin, a fraction of the contract value, so price changes on the full value produce large percentage gains or losses on capital. Losses are not capped at the margin; further mark-to-market calls can arise."], ["Trading Mechanism", "Two traders both close out existing positions against each other: one sells to close a long, the other buys to close a short. Open interest will:", ["Decrease", "Increase", "Remain unchanged", "Double"], 0, "Open interest counts outstanding contracts. When both sides are closing existing positions, one contract is extinguished, so open interest falls. If both were opening new positions, it would rise."], ["Introduction to Options", "Gamma of an option is usually highest when the option is:", ["Deep out of the money with long time to expiry", "Deep in the money with long time to expiry", "At the money and close to expiry", "Far out of the money and close to expiry"], 2, "Gamma measures how fast delta changes. Near-the-money options close to expiry have deltas that swing sharply between 0 and 1 for small price moves, so their gamma peaks. Deep ITM or OTM options have stable deltas."], ["Trading Mechanism", "Trades in the equity derivatives segment of NSE are matched on the basis of:", ["Size of order only", "Alphabetical order of member codes", "Random selection by the system", "Price-time priority"], 3, "The order-matching system gives priority first to the best price and, at the same price, to the order entered earlier. This transparent rule applies to all members and ensures fair execution."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "Position limits at the client level in equity derivatives aim mainly to:", ["Stop any one client from building a price-distorting position", "Ensure that every client makes a profit", "Fix a minimum number of contracts a client must trade", "Restrict derivatives trading to large institutional investors only"], 0, "Client-level limits cap concentration, reducing the risk of manipulation and of large defaults. They do not guarantee profits, set minimum trading or bar retail clients."], ["Introduction to Options", "Just before an important company announcement, implied volatility on its options rises sharply. Other things equal, this will:", ["Increase the premiums of both calls and puts", "Increase call premiums but reduce put premiums", "Reduce the premiums of both calls and puts", "Leave premiums unchanged because the stock price has not moved"], 0, "Higher volatility raises the probability of large moves in either direction, increasing the value of both calls and puts. This sensitivity to volatility is measured by vega, which is positive for long calls and long puts."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "Price discovery as a function of derivatives markets refers to:", ["The exchange fixing the price of the underlying at the start of each month", "Derivatives prices reflecting expectations about future spot prices", "SEBI announcing a fair value for every listed derivatives contract", "The issuer of the underlying deciding the price of its own futures"], 1, "Active trading by many participants with different views makes derivatives prices reflect collective expectations about the underlying. Neither the exchange, SEBI nor the issuer sets these prices administratively."], ["Strategies using Equity Futures and Options", "A trader buys a ₹500 call at ₹20 and a ₹500 put at ₹18, same expiry. What are his break-even prices at expiry?", ["₹480 and ₹520", "₹482 and ₹518", "₹462 and ₹520", "₹462 and ₹538"], 3, "Total premium = 20 + 18 = ₹38. Break-evens of a long straddle are strike ± total premium: 500 − 38 = ₹462 and 500 + 38 = ₹538. Using only one leg's premium gives the wrong figures."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "Why are swaps generally classified as OTC derivatives rather than exchange-traded ones?", ["They can be traded only by the Reserve Bank of India", "They never involve exchange of any cash flows between parties", "They are always settled by physical delivery of the underlying", "Their terms are privately negotiated between two parties"], 3, "Swaps are bilateral agreements whose notional, tenor and payment terms are tailored to the parties' needs, which is the hallmark of OTC contracts. They do involve exchange of cash flows, usually net, not physical delivery."], ["Clearing and Settlement System", "A trader's short option position faces a sharp rise in volatility. Which margin is most likely to increase?", ["The premium paid on his long option positions", "The settlement guarantee fund contribution of the exchange", "The stamp duty on the contract", "Initial margin on the short option"], 3, "Risk-based initial margins on short options rise when volatility increases, since potential losses widen. Premiums on longs are already paid, and stamp duty does not depend on volatility."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "Stamp duty on equity derivatives trades in India is:", ["Paid by the seller to the stock exchange as a membership fee", "Collected centrally through exchanges and paid by the buyer", "Collected by SEBI as a regulatory fee", "Not applicable to derivatives transactions at all"], 1, "Under the amended Indian Stamp Act, stamp duty on securities transactions, including derivatives, is collected by exchanges or clearing corporations on behalf of states and is payable by the buyer."], ["Sales Practices and Investor Protection", "An investor's complaint against a broker is not resolved satisfactorily. A next step available to him is to:", ["File the complaint with the Insurance Ombudsman", "Approach the company whose shares he traded", "Ask the Reserve Bank of India to reverse the disputed trade", "Escalate on SCORES and use online dispute resolution"], 3, "Securities market complaints can be lodged on SCORES and pursued through conciliation and arbitration on the ODR portal. Insurance ombudsman, the issuer company and RBI are not the right forums."], ["Introduction to Forwards and Futures", "An investor holding a stock portfolio sells index futures equal to its beta-adjusted value. If the market falls 5%, the hedge is intended to:", ["Offset portfolio losses with gains on the short futures", "Double the gain made on the portfolio", "Convert the whole portfolio into a risk-free bond paying 5%", "Eliminate company-specific risk in the stocks held"], 0, "A short index futures hedge gains when the market falls, offsetting the market-related loss on the portfolio. It removes systematic exposure only; stock-specific risk not explained by beta remains."], ["Introduction to Forwards and Futures", "A stock trades at ₹1,000 and its two-month futures trade at ₹1,012. Ignoring dividends, what annualised simple interest rate is implied in the futures price?", ["7.2% per annum", "1.2% per annum", "6.0% per annum", "14.4% per annum"], 0, "Implied two-month return = 12 ÷ 1,000 = 1.2%. Annualised simply, 1.2% × 12/2 = 7.2% per annum. 1.2% is the unannualised figure; 14.4% wrongly annualises by multiplying by 12."], ["Strategies using Equity Futures and Options", "An investor expects a stock to fall modestly but wants to limit the cost of the position. Which strategy suits this view best?", ["Long straddle", "Bear put spread", "Bull call spread", "Short strangle"], 1, "A bear put spread (buy higher-strike put, sell lower-strike put) profits from a moderate fall at lower cost than a single put. A straddle bets on volatility, a bull spread on a rise, and a short strangle on a quiet market."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "Which of the following is an example of a derivative whose underlying is not a financial asset?", ["An equity share of an oil company", "A Treasury Bill issued by the RBI", "A futures contract on crude oil", "A unit of a liquid mutual fund scheme"], 2, "A crude oil future derives its value from a physical commodity. An equity share, a T-Bill and a mutual fund unit are themselves primary securities, not contracts deriving value from something else."], ["Trading Mechanism", "Which entity places orders on the exchange trading system on behalf of clients?", ["A trading member", "The clearing corporation", "The depository participant", "The registrar and share transfer agent"], 0, "Trading members (brokers) are authorised to enter orders on the exchange. The clearing corporation clears and settles trades, depositories hold securities, and RTAs handle issuer registers."], ["Trading Mechanism", "A client wants to trade equity derivatives through a broker. Which of the following is mandatory before trading?", ["Depositing the full contract value of every trade with the exchange", "Completing KYC and signing the required risk disclosure documents", "Obtaining a separate registration from SEBI as a derivatives trader", "Holding at least ₹10 lakh of shares in a demat account"], 1, "Clients must complete KYC and receive risk disclosure documents and rights and obligations before trading derivatives. They pay margins, not full contract value, and need no separate SEBI registration."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "Which measure is designed by SEBI and exchanges to monitor and restrain excessive speculative positions in derivatives?", ["A fixed guaranteed return on every derivatives contract", "Market-wide position limits and surveillance alerts", "A ban on all retail clients from trading options", "Compulsory physical delivery of index futures"], 1, "Market-wide position limits, surveillance mechanisms and related measures monitor build-up of positions. They do not guarantee returns, ban retail clients outright, or require physical delivery of index contracts."], ["Understanding Index", "An index had a base value of 1,000 and its current free-float market cap is 25 times the base-period free-float market cap. Ignoring divisor adjustments, the index value is:", ["25,000", "2,500", "26,000", "24,000"], 0, "Index value = base value × (current market cap ÷ base market cap) = 1,000 × 25 = 25,000. The ratio of market caps is applied multiplicatively to the base value, not added to it."], ["Sales Practices and Investor Protection", "Before allowing a client to trade in derivatives, a broker should verify the client's:", ["Financial standing and risk-bearing ability", "Political affiliation and religious views", "Choice of mobile phone operator and internet provider", "Preference between NSE and BSE indices"], 0, "Brokers must gather information on income, net worth and experience to judge whether derivatives are appropriate. Unrelated personal details have no bearing on suitability."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "For a resident individual, a loss from trading equity futures on a recognised exchange can generally be set off against:", ["Only salary income of the same year", "Long-term capital gains of the next decade without limit", "Agricultural income only", "Other income except salary, in the same year"], 3, "Exchange-traded F&O is treated as non-speculative business. Its loss can be set off against other heads except salary in the same year and carried forward against business income for up to eight years."], ["Sales Practices and Investor Protection", "A stock broker learns that a client's position will receive a large order from the broker's institutional client and trades ahead of it for its own account. This is called:", ["Hedging", "Front running", "Arbitrage", "Rebalancing"], 1, "Trading ahead of a known pending client order to benefit from the expected price impact is front running, a prohibited practice. Hedging and arbitrage are legitimate strategies."], ["Sales Practices and Investor Protection", "A broker's representative tells a new client that a particular options strategy is 'risk-free and sure to earn 5% a month'. This is:", ["Acceptable if the client is experienced", "Permitted when the strategy has worked in the past", "A misleading statement that violates the code of conduct", "Allowed if it is said orally and not in writing"], 2, "Guaranteeing returns or describing leveraged options as risk-free misrepresents risk and breaches the broker code of conduct. No derivatives strategy can promise fixed monthly returns."], ["Introduction to Options", "A trader holds long call options covering 2,000 shares, each with a delta of 0.6. To make the position delta neutral, he should:", ["Sell 1,200 shares of the underlying", "Buy 1,200 shares of the underlying", "Sell 2,000 shares of the underlying", "Buy 800 shares of the underlying"], 0, "Position delta = 2,000 × 0.6 = +1,200 share-equivalents. To neutralise it, he must sell 1,200 shares. Buying would increase the delta, and selling 2,000 shares would over-hedge."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "Mark-to-market losses paid daily on an open futures position are accounted for by the client as:", ["A debit to a mark-to-market account", "An increase in the value of shares held", "A loan given to the clearing corporation", "Revenue from operations"], 0, "Daily MTM payments represent realised or notional changes in the value of the futures position and are recorded in an MTM account, which is closed to profit and loss on squaring off or at year-end per accounting standards."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "Disputes between a client and a trading member relating to derivatives trades can be resolved through:", ["The Reserve Bank's Integrated Ombudsman Scheme for banks", "The Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority", "The exchange's grievance cell and SEBI's ODR platform", "The company whose shares are the underlying"], 2, "Client complaints against brokers are handled through the exchange's grievance system, SCORES and the Online Dispute Resolution (ODR) platform with conciliation and arbitration. The banking ombudsman and IRDAI do not cover these disputes."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "Which law gives SEBI the power to regulate the business in stock exchanges and protect investors' interests?", ["The Banking Regulation Act, 1949", "The Foreign Exchange Management Act, 1999", "The Indian Contract Act, 1872", "The Securities and Exchange Board of India Act, 1992"], 3, "The SEBI Act, 1992 established SEBI and gives it powers to regulate securities markets and protect investors. The Banking Regulation Act concerns banks, FEMA concerns foreign exchange, and the Contract Act covers general contracts."], ["Strategies using Equity Futures and Options", "Which strategy profits most if the underlying ends exactly at the middle strike at expiry?", ["Long strangle", "Protective put", "Long butterfly spread", "Short futures"], 2, "A long butterfly (buy one low strike, sell two middle strikes, buy one high strike) has its peak payoff at the middle strike. A long strangle needs a large move, a protective put protects against falls, and short futures profits from falls."], ["Clearing and Settlement System", "A trader is short a stock call option and it is exercised at expiry. Which process determines that he must fulfil the obligation?", ["Novation by the trading member", "Exercise by the option seller", "Assignment by the clearing corporation", "Netting by the depository"], 2, "When long option holders exercise, the clearing corporation randomly assigns the obligation to short positions. Exercise is the buyer's right; assignment is how sellers are chosen to meet it."], ["Clearing and Settlement System", "Initial margin in the equity derivatives segment is designed to cover:", ["The full contract value of the position, payable on the trade date", "Potential one-day losses on a position under adverse price and volatility scenarios", "The brokerage and taxes payable on the trade", "Losses arising only on the expiry day"], 1, "Initial margin is set using a risk-based model that estimates the largest likely one-day loss under various scenarios. It is a fraction of contract value, sized to protect the clearing corporation."], ["Clearing and Settlement System", "A professional clearing member (PCM) in the derivatives segment:", ["Trades only on its own account and clears its own trades", "Acts as a depository that holds all derivatives positions of clients", "Clears trades of trading members but does not trade itself", "Is a type of client that uses multiple brokers"], 2, "A PCM is a clearing-only entity, often a bank or custodian, that clears and settles trades executed by trading members or custodial participants. It does not have trading rights of its own."], ["Understanding Index", "Which of these is NOT a common use of a stock market index?", ["Benchmarking the performance of fund managers", "Serving as the underlying for index futures and options", "Creating index funds and exchange-traded funds", "Fixing the issue price of a company's IPO"], 3, "Indices are used to benchmark portfolios, as underlyings for derivatives and as the basis for passive products. IPO prices are discovered through book building or fixed by the issuer with the lead manager, not by an index."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "Securities Transaction Tax on an equity futures trade is levied on:", ["The buyer, on the margin amount paid", "The seller, on the traded value of the futures contract", "Both parties, on the brokerage paid", "The clearing corporation, on open interest"], 1, "STT on futures applies to the sell side on the traded value. It is not computed on margin or brokerage. The exact rate is notified in the Finance Act and has been revised from time to time."], ["Trading Mechanism", "Why are stocks admitted to the derivatives segment required to meet criteria on liquidity, market cap and trading volume?", ["To guarantee that those stocks will give positive returns", "To limit derivatives trading to government-owned companies", "To ensure that each stock pays regular dividends", "To ensure the underlying is liquid and hard to manipulate"], 3, "Eligibility norms ensure the underlying cash market is deep and widely traded, making price manipulation harder and arbitrage and hedging feasible. They say nothing about future returns or dividends."], ["Understanding Index", "In a price-weighted index, which stock has the greatest influence on index movements?", ["The stock with the highest share price", "The stock with the largest market capitalisation", "The stock with the highest trading volume", "The stock with the most free-float shares"], 0, "In a price-weighted index, each stock's weight depends on its price, so high-priced shares move the index most regardless of company size. Market-cap based indices instead give larger companies more weight."], ["Strategies using Equity Futures and Options", "A trader buys a ₹1,000 call at ₹30 and sells a ₹1,050 call at ₹12, same stock and expiry. What is his maximum profit per share?", ["₹50", "₹18", "₹32", "₹42"], 2, "Net debit = 30 − 12 = ₹18. Maximum profit = difference in strikes − net debit = 50 − 18 = ₹32, earned if the stock ends at or above ₹1,050. ₹18 is the maximum loss, not the profit."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "Which body grants approval for a stock exchange to start trading in a new type of derivatives contract?", ["The Ministry of Corporate Affairs", "The Reserve Bank of India", "SEBI", "The Institute of Chartered Accountants of India"], 2, "Stock exchanges need SEBI's approval to introduce derivative products. MCA administers company law, RBI governs money and forex markets, and ICAI regulates accountants."], ["Understanding Index", "A company has total market capitalisation of ₹40,000 crore and its promoters hold 60% of the shares. If all other shares are free-float, what is its free-float market capitalisation?", ["₹24,000 crore", "₹16,000 crore", "₹40,000 crore", "₹10,000 crore"], 1, "Free float excludes promoter holding, so the free-float portion is 40% of shares. Free-float market cap = 40% × ₹40,000 crore = ₹16,000 crore. ₹24,000 crore is the promoter portion, not the free float."], ["Introduction to Options", "A stock trades at ₹1,240 and a call option with a strike of ₹1,200 is priced at ₹62. What is the time value of the option?", ["₹40", "₹62", "₹22", "₹102"], 2, "Intrinsic value of the call = 1,240 − 1,200 = ₹40. Time value = premium − intrinsic value = 62 − 40 = ₹22. ₹40 is the intrinsic value, not the time value."], ["Introduction to Options", "A put option with a strike of ₹500 is said to be out of the money when the underlying stock price is:", ["₹470", "₹500", "₹450", "₹530"], 3, "A put is out of the money when the spot price is above the strike, since exercising would mean selling below market. At ₹470 or ₹450 the put is in the money, and at ₹500 it is at the money."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "A stock broker in the derivatives segment must maintain client funds:", ["In its own proprietary account to earn interest", "In any bank account it chooses, as long as the client is informed later", "With the clearing corporation only on expiry days", "Separately, using them only for that client's obligations"], 3, "SEBI requires segregation of client funds from the broker's own money; using one client's money for another client or for the broker's own trading is prohibited."], ["Introduction to Forwards and Futures", "Which feature is found in forward contracts but not in exchange-traded futures?", ["Daily mark-to-market settlement through a clearing corporation", "Standardised lot sizes and expiry dates", "Guarantee of performance by a clearing corporation", "Terms such as quantity and delivery date tailored to the two parties"], 3, "Forwards are private, customised agreements. Futures are standardised exchange contracts with daily mark-to-market and a clearing corporation guarantee, which is why futures carry far less counterparty risk."], ["Introduction to Forwards and Futures", "A portfolio manager holds equity worth ₹5 crore with beta 0.9 and wants to hedge fully using index futures. Each contract has a value of ₹15 lakh. Approximately how many contracts should she sell?", ["33 contracts", "27 contracts", "36 contracts", "30 contracts"], 3, "Contracts = portfolio value × beta ÷ contract value = 5,00,00,000 × 0.9 ÷ 15,00,000 = 30. Ignoring beta gives about 33, which would over-hedge a portfolio that is less volatile than the index."], ["Understanding Index", "An index fund manager's portfolio returned 11.4% while the benchmark index returned 12.0%. The 0.6% difference is best described as:", ["Alpha generated by superior stock selection", "Tracking difference due to costs", "Impact cost of the index constituents", "Basis between the index and its futures"], 1, "An index fund aims to match its benchmark; the gap between fund and index return is tracking difference, caused by expenses, cash holdings and replication lags. It is negative here, so it is not alpha."], ["Introduction to Options", "Which option position has a gain potential limited to the premium received but a potentially large loss?", ["Long put", "Short put", "Long call", "Long straddle"], 1, "A put writer earns at most the premium, while losses grow as the stock falls, up to the strike minus premium. Long option positions risk only the premium paid and have large profit potential."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "A broker repeatedly places buy and sell orders between related accounts to create a false appearance of trading activity in a stock option. This is prohibited under:", ["SEBI's regulations governing the operations of mutual funds", "SEBI's PFUTP regulations on fraudulent trade practices", "The Companies Act provisions on share buyback", "The RBI's guidelines on call money market"], 1, "Creating artificial volume through circular or synthetic trades is a manipulative practice under SEBI (PFUTP) Regulations. It misleads other participants and attracts penalties and debarment."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "A speculator differs from a hedger mainly because the speculator:", ["Always holds both the derivative and the underlying asset", "Is prohibited from trading in exchange-traded derivatives", "Takes on price risk without an exposure to protect", "Uses only options and never trades futures contracts"], 2, "Speculators take on risk in the hope of profiting from price movements; they do not have an underlying exposure to protect. They may use futures or options and are an important source of liquidity in exchange markets."], ["Understanding Index", "When a constituent stock of a market-cap weighted index is replaced by another stock, the index provider adjusts the divisor so that:", ["The index value is reset to its original base value", "The new stock gets the same weight as the stock it replaced", "The index value rises by the new stock's market capitalisation", "The index value does not change merely because of the replacement"], 3, "Divisor adjustments keep the index continuous so that only market price movements, not changes in composition or corporate actions, change the index level. Weights still depend on each stock's own free-float market cap."], ["Sales Practices and Investor Protection", "Which practice best demonstrates fair treatment of a retail derivatives client?", ["Highlighting only past winning trades of other clients", "Encouraging the client to borrow money to increase position size", "Avoiding discussion of losses so as not to discourage him", "Explaining margin calls and that losses can exceed capital"], 3, "Fair dealing requires balanced disclosure of risks, including margin calls and losses beyond the initial deposit. Cherry-picked successes and encouraging borrowing for speculation are poor sales practices."], ["Clearing and Settlement System", "If a clearing member fails to meet its settlement obligation, the first resource the clearing corporation uses is typically:", ["The Core Settlement Guarantee Fund", "Contributions from non-defaulting clients", "A loan from the Reserve Bank of India", "The defaulting member's own margins and collateral"], 3, "In the default waterfall, the defaulter's margins and deposits are used first. Only if these are insufficient does the clearing corporation draw on the settlement guarantee fund and other resources."]]};
